# Entity-Specific Field Names

Small entities that look like `_id` + `name` should still use **names that stay clear when combined**. Generic labels (`name`, `title` only when it is truly a title, bare `id` aliases) collide the moment two projections sit on one object or in one log line.

## Display / lookup fields

Prefer `[entity]_name` (or a domain-specific word: `iana_zone`, `role_name`) over `name`.

```xml
<!-- Prefer: still unique when Widget.Reference and Account.Reference are combined -->
<field type="string" ... friendlyName="Widget Name">widget_name</field>
<field type="string" ... friendlyName="Account Name">account_name</field>

<!-- Avoid on lookup-style entities: two `name` fields, no idea which entity -->
<field type="string" ... friendlyName="Name">name</field>
```

Same idea for other generic nouns (`code`, `label`, `value` on a typed lookup): prefix or pick a word that cannot mean two entities.

## Id aliases

Keep `_id` as the primary key. Add an `idAlias="true"` field named `[entity]_id` (or the route name, e.g. `jurisdiction_id`) so callers, FKs, and logs read with a noun as the domain grows. The generator copies `_id` onto the alias on insert/replace. `tenant="Route"` URLs use the alias segment (`:jurisdiction_id`), not `:_id`.

```xml
<field type="Uuid" isNullable="false" friendlyName="Id">_id</field>
<field idAlias="true" type="Uuid" ... friendlyName="Widget Id">widget_id</field>
```

Foreign keys on other entities should use that same noun (`widget_id`), not a second generic `id`.
